    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Public Health",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4972\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act directing the commissioner of health to conduct a study of the\npractice of obstetrical ultrasound procedures for non-medical and\nnon-diagnostic purposes\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis legislation would direct the Commissioner of Health to conduct a\nstudy analyzing the practice of conducting prolonged and/or\nunnecessary ultrasounds for non-medical and non-diagnostic purposes\nfrom both a medical and business perspective.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill requires the commissioner of health to conduct a\nstudy analyzing the impact of conducting prolonged and/or unnecessary\nultrasounds for non-medical and non-diagnostic purposes on the health\nof the receiving individual, as well as analyzing the proliferation of\nbusiness offering such services and the adequacy of the current\nrequirements for maintenance and operation of ultrasound machines.\n\nSection 2 of the bill provides that the act shall take effect\nimmediately.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nObstetrical ultrasound procedures are an immensely useful medical tool\nto ensure the health of a mother and her fetus during pregnancy. When\nused for medical procedures, ultrasounds are considered to be safe and\nnon-invasive, and help to confirm a pregnancy, determine whether\nvaginal bleeding is occurring, and monitor a fetus' heartbeat.\n\nHowever, despite its usefulness, an ultrasound is still a procedure\nthat should be administered by a licensed medical professional and\nshould not be commercialized for the purposes of recording fetal\n\"keepsake\" videos and images. The FDA has released a statement urging\npeople not to use ultrasound devices in this manner, as it is unclear\nwhat impact prolonged exposure to these devices might have on the\nhealth of the fetus and the mother. The FDA notes that ultrasounds can\nproduce slight tissue heating and cavitation, the long-term health\nimpacts of which are currently unknown.\n\nThis legislation would direct the commissioner of health to conduct a\nstudy of this practice, and determine the health impacts of the\npractice along with the proliferation of these kind of \"keepsake\"\nimaging businesses in the State of New York.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state.\n\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
